            {
              "id": "5c87a57a-9c32-4f1c-9a73-4450216b76cf",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": "<em><strong>The Player of Games</strong></em> was written by Iain M. Banks, originally published Macmillan in 1988."
            },
            {
              "id": "527c6e8c-1eb9-499f-a1e7-c66350ed019d",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": ""
            },
            {
              "id": "eeaf08a7-70fa-4caf-b779-46dc51da18ef",
              "type": "quote",
              "html": "<em>The Culture — a human/machine symbiotic society — has thrown up many great Game Players, and one of the greatest is Gurgeh Jernau Morat Gurgeh. The Player of Games. Master of every board, computer and strategy.</em><em><strong><br><br></strong></em><em>Bored with success, Gurgeh travels to the Empire of Azad, cruel and incredibly wealthy, to try their fabulous game. . . a game so complex, so like life itself, that the winner becomes emperor. Mocked, blackmailed, almost murdered, Gurgeh accepts the game, and with it the challenge of his life — and very possibly his death.</em>"
            }
          ]
        },
